--- !u!114 &463844253
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 463844252}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e8ea7bc5a80b1b54da0ca2178b6fd5f5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  DialogueOptions:
  - "\u201CHola se\xF1ora, you are more than welcome in my shop. No need to be so
    fearful, the abolishment means that you can enter the same as any other.\u201D "
  - "\"You said that it\u2019s the same <link=hacendados><color=#1A4D8F>hacendados</color></link>?
    Do you mean within the coffee <link=haciendas><color=#1A4D8F>haciendas</color></link>
    as well?\" "
  - "\u201CYou mean that even now, you have no faith that you\u2019ll eventually
    be free?\u201D "
  AnswerOptions:
  - "<i>The Aprendiz looks through the window, peeking inside with caution, as if
    fearful of a sudden blow to come down as punishment for the crime of daring to
    look into your shop. After a few moments, she builds up the courage to slowly
    open the door, still looking at you as if you\u2019d suddenly lash out. Finally,
    once she\u2019s managed to step inside with no harm to her person, she squeaks
    out a small, \u201Cbuenos d\xEDas, se\xF1ora.\u201D, curtseying shyly in your
    direction.</i> "
  - '"Peh, so they say. Though now they call us apprentices, we are still bound to
    the <link=hacienda><color=#1A4D8F>hacienda</color></link> - more so to the very
    same <link=hacendados><color=#1A4D8F>hacendados</color></link> that ruled our
    lives before. Women and girls still work the beans, carry water, and tend to
    the young. Different name, yet control stays in the same hands." '
  - '"<link=si_claro><color=#1A4D8F>Si, claro</color></link>. With sugar and before
    the abolishment, there were many more of us, as you would expect. But even now,
    with coffee, labor is still needed, and the same greedy <link=asquerosos><color=#1A4D8F>asquerosos</color></link>
    are still in charge." '
  - "\u201CFor a time freedom, for me, for us, was more than just a word. The libreta,
    the contracts, they made sure it would never be more than just a word.\u201D"
  SourceBlurbs:
  - "Coffee production depended on enslaved women for fieldwork, processing, and
    domestic tasks; with this, the gendered nature of forced labor is made apparent.
    And after their collective emancipation in 1873, many formerly enslaved people
    in Puerto Rico were forced into apprenticeship contracts that decisively restricted
    their freedom and mobility, even beyond that of the similarly situated j\xEDbaro\u2019s.
    Despite their freedom on paper and in law, in actual practice ongoing economic
    dependence and forced labor contracts continued to limit their autonomy even
    after the abolishment (Fern\xE1ndez-Sacco, 2020; Garc\xEDa, 1989; Scarano, 1984;
    Bergad, 1983). "
  - Before abolishment, there was a heavy reliance on enslaved workers for labor,
    especially in haciendas where specialized skills were necessary for the best
    results. Due to this, even after abolition, former slaves were often bound to
    an informal version of slavery, specifically labor arrangements that tied them
    to their previous work and the people they worked under for as long as three
    years more(Scarano, 1984; Bergad, 1983).
  - "Freedom for enslaved people, even after abolition, was restricted heavily by
    both economic and legal structures. The transition from slavery to apprentice
    labor reproduced many of the same conditions faced during the enslaved peoples'
    times of enslavement, highlighting a horrific continuity in exploitation of the
    lower classes(Fern\xE1ndez-Sacco, 2020; Pic\xF3, 1979)."
  AnswerVoiceClips: []
  PathingPoints: {fileID: 0}
  IsTalking: 0
  StopsToLookInWindow: 1
  StartingPosition: 0
  Name: Aprendiz
  moveSpeed: 2
  exclamationMark: {fileID: 1521053957}
