  m_TagString: Untagged
  m_Icon: {fileID: 0}
  m_NavMeshLayer: 0
  m_StaticEditorFlags: 0
  m_IsActive: 1
--- !u!114 &1232052102
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1232052101}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e8ea7bc5a80b1b54da0ca2178b6fd5f5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  DialogueOptions:
  - "\u201CYou seem tired. What\u2019s weighing on your mind?\u201D"
  - "\u201CSurely you\u2019re not alone, do you not have any workers to help?\u201D"
  - "\u201CDoes that mean that you don\u2019t think any of it was worth it?\u201D "
  AnswerOptions:
  - "<i>The cafetalero quietly opens the door, walking inside with an air of solemnity.
    He looks around, his tired eyes clearly taking in the space. He comes to the
    counter and hesitates before a quiet, \u201CBuenas, se\xF1ora\u201D comes out.
    </i> "
  - "\u201CThe same thing I think of constantly; I am a man drowning in debt, and
    it is solely because I dared to dream of bigger things. I took credit from a
    merchant in Ponce with stars in my eyes, and since then, the only gain I\u2019ve
    made is that of my debt with each year that passes.\u201D "
  - '"<link=jornaleros><color=#1A4D8F>Jornaleros</color></link> came during harvest,
    crowds of them in fact. But after coffee prices fell, I could no longer afford
    to pay them." '
  - '"<link=si_y_no><color=#1A4D8F>Si, y no</color></link>. For just a fleeting moment,
    it was worth it. The beans reached Europe and we were paid handsomely. It was
    a fine dream. But then, one bad storm, and suddenly all that profit disappeared,
    and we were left with nothing but crippling debt." '
  SourceBlurbs:
  - "Cafetaleros{coffee growers}, small and medium, had a heavy reliance on merchant
    credit, often using their future harvests as a guarantor. This created cycles
    of debt that made them victims to the whims of market fluctuations at the time
    (D\xEDaz, 1982; Bergad, 1983)."
  - "Cafetaleros{coffee growers} relied on seasonal jornaleros{day laborers} for
    their harvests, but the livelihoods of the jornaleros{day laborers} were inextricably
    tied to the coffee market, which was incredibly volatile. This further emphasized
    the dependence of the rural community on plantation structures. (Bergad, 1983;
    Pic\xF3, 1993)."
  - "The economic payout for small growers was incredibly fragile. Droughts, storms,
    or market shifts could summarily wipe out any previous profit, demonstrating
    just how precarious agrarian capitalism could be (Pic\xF3, 1993; Bergad, 1983)."
  AnswerVoiceClips: []
  PathingPoints: {fileID: 0}
  IsTalking: 0
  StopsToLookInWindow: 0
  StartingPosition: 2
  Name: Cafetalero
  moveSpeed: 2
  exclamationMark: {fileID: 896106289}
