--- !u!114 &1722135215
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1722135214}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e8ea7bc5a80b1b54da0ca2178b6fd5f5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  DialogueOptions:
  - "\u201CJust a few reales? For an entire day's worth of labor?\u201D "
  - "\u201CThat\u2019s awful. Do you at least own land? Perhaps you could begin your
    own trade?\u201D "
  - "\u201CYou make it sound as if coffee has changed everything.\u201D "
  AnswerOptions:
  - "<i>The J\xEDbaro enters, looking bent and worn. He looks curiously around your
    shop, visibly awed, as he timidly calls out, \u201Chola?\u201D. He is visibly
    relieved when you call him over, though he still seems unsure, especially when
    he takes in the knick-knacks and coffee cups you have stationed across the counter.
    </i> <br><br>  J\xEDbaro: \u201CYour coffee cups look so finely crafted, it caught
    my eye as soon as I came in. I\u2019d love to have a cup <link=mi_hija><color=#1A4D8F>mi
    hija</color></link>, I\u2019d never get a chance otherwise; for me, it is work
    from dawn to dusk, breaking my back for just a few reales.\u201D "
  - "\u201CEy, we can thank the libreta for that; it\u2019s bound us to poor pay
    and terrible conditions. I was hopeful that maybe this was the time to improve
    my lot; however, without a patron\u2019s signature, no one would hire me. I was
    trapped, and so this is where I\u2019ve ended up.\u201D "
  - '"Si, once, I did. But then, our lands were passed to the <link=hacendados><color=#1A4D8F>hacendados</color></link>.
    We became <link=jornaleros><color=#1A4D8F>jornaleros</color></link> on land that
    was originally ours, and now we own nothing. We are barely more than <link=esclavos><color=#1A4D8F>esclavos</color></link>,
    the only difference is that at least we can go to our homes at the end of the
    day." '
  - "\"It did, in a way. Coffee brought wealth untold and attention to <link=la_isla><color=#1A4D8F>la
    isla</color></link> that I could have never even imagined\u2026 but that good
    fortune is not for us, <link=mi_hija><color=#1A4D8F>mi hija</color></link>. Upon
    our backs, their riches are built, and our standing beneath them is further cemented.\" "
  SourceBlurbs:
  - "The libreta system was used in a way that forced rural workers to labor only
    for individual employers, limiting their mobility and impeding their bargaining
    power. Workers could not look for or even be considered for employment without
    the signature of their patron, which in turn manufactured a near-permanent dependency
    on the upper class employers and limited personal freedom of the j\xEDbaro\u2019s(Gazeta
    de Puerto Rico, 1849; Pic\xF3, Libertad y Servidumbre, 1979). "
  - "The libreta system was used in a way that forced rural workers to labor only
    for individual employers, limiting their mobility and impeding their bargaining
    power. Workers could not look for or even be considered for employment without
    the signature of their patron, which in turn manufactured a near-permanent dependency
    on the upper class employers and limited personal freedom of the j\xEDbaro\u2019s(Gazeta
    de Puerto Rico, 1849; Pic\xF3, Libertad y Servidumbre, 1979)."
  - "The Puerto Rican coffee boom may have created more wealth for planters, hacendados{plantation
    owners} and merchants but for the lower class j\xEDbaro\u2019s, it just further
    deepened institutional inequality. Many j\xEDbaro\u2019s remained in tenuous
    positions of labor, generating more wealth for the very people that were steadily
    removing their access to land, credit, and independence (Pico, 1983; Bergad,
    1983). "
  AnswerVoiceClips:
  - {fileID: 0}
  PathingPoints: {fileID: 0}
  IsTalking: 0
  StopsToLookInWindow: 0
  StartingPosition: 2
  Name: "J\xEDbaro"
  moveSpeed: 2
  exclamationMark: {fileID: 1467111170}
