--- !u!114 &1809972621
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1809972620}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e8ea7bc5a80b1b54da0ca2178b6fd5f5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  DialogueOptions:
  - '"You seemed familiar with the coffee beans, and you have the mannerisms of the
    upper class. Are you perhaps a <link=hacendado><color=#1A4D8F>hacendado</color></link>?" '
  - "\u201CWhere do you sell that coffee to be making such a profit?\u201D  "
  - "\u201CWould you say that coffee has brought a change to Puerto Rico, in that
    you\u2019re able to make such a profit from it?\u201D "
  AnswerOptions:
  - "<i>The hacendado walks into your shop with no hesitation, their steps sure and
    their face a mask of confidence. They walk straight to your counter, eyes quickly
    taking stock of your surroundings before eventually snagging on the coffee beans
    on the shelf behind you, which brings a smirk to their face. They offer a concise,
    \u201CBuenas tardes se\xF1orita.\u201D </i>"
  - "\"Si, que bueno se\xF1ora! I\u2019m pleased that you were able to make that
    assumption based on so little. Yes, as you said, I am a <link=hacendado><color=#1A4D8F>hacendado</color></link>,
    one with a great many workers below me. Thanks to them, I have been able to amass
    fortunes untold.\" "
  - '"Many places; to name a few Spain, France, Europe. The beans our <link=haciendas><color=#1A4D8F>haciendas</color></link>
    produce are of great quality, and fetch us a great profit." '
  - '"<link=claro_que_si><color=#1A4D8F>Claro que si</color></link>! Coffee has elevated
    my people and I to men of standing, and has changed <link=la_isla><color=#1A4D8F>la
    isla</color></link> from a no name speck in the ocean to an island that houses
    some of the best coffee in the world!" '
  SourceBlurbs:
  - "Post-abolition plantations had a heavy reliance on labor provided by a mixture
    of jornaleros, and apprentices. Using legal and economic mechanisms available
    at the time, hierarchical control was reinforced upon the lower classes as the
    hacendados{plantation owners} continued to run their plantations (Pic\xF3, 1979;
    Bergad, 1980)."
  - "Puerto Rican coffee became highly sought after within European markets, thereby
    connecting the island\u2019s economy to transatlantic trade networks. This connection
    brought with it great fortune for the elites and upper class, yet the laborers
    often gained little themselves. (D\xEDaz, 1982; Bergad, 1983)."
  - "Coffee was the foundation of the creation of a new elite class and the structure
    that brought Puerto Rico upwards in the global economy, while at the same time
    reinforcing local labor hierarchies and property concentration (Bergad, 1980;
    Pic\xF3, 1983)."
  AnswerVoiceClips: []
  PathingPoints: {fileID: 0}
  IsTalking: 0
  StopsToLookInWindow: 0
  StartingPosition: 2
  Name: Hacendado
  moveSpeed: 3.5
  exclamationMark: {fileID: 563605655}
